@title Do Machines Struggle Where Humans Do?
@kind paper
@source paper:LLM and Human Comprehension of Obfuscated Code
@cite J. V. Le†, A. Nguyen†, T. Nguyen (2026). Do Machines Struggle Where Humans Do? LLM and Human Comprehension of Obfuscated Code. arXiv:2606.31725 [cs.SE]. Submitted to FSE.
@arxiv 2606.31725
@link https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.31725 Read the paper
@link ../../assets/documents/publications/2026SPLASH-SRC.pdf Two-page SRC abstract
@authors Jack V. Le† and Anh Nguyen† (co-first), Tien Nguyen
@note Dialogue is dramatized. Figures marked § are quoted from the source. The Model is a composite of the reasoning-tuned regime, not any one system.
@status published
@verify
@palette slate
@include _facts/obfuscation.vn
@cast Jack hue=210 glasses
@cast Model name="a reasoning-tuned model" lattice=sparse hue=192
@cast You player hue=20

@bg lab
@show Jack left
Jack: Obfuscation is almost always measured without people, on two untested assumptions: that difficulty is monotonic, and that what burdens a person burdens a model. ^§1
@card Five tiers | L0: original, name masked | L1: uninformative renaming | L1b: adversarial renaming | L2: control-flow flattening | L3: both
Jack: {n_humans} CS undergraduates, a {session_min}-minute supervised session, twelve output-prediction questions each: {n_answers} answers with paired response times. {n_models} language models on the same tiers.
@thumb
Jack (smile): Before the numbers, place a bet.
* Harder tier, lower accuracy. Always. -> bet_monotonic
* It depends on the language. -> bet_language

== bet_monotonic
@show You right
You: More obfuscation, more pain. A straight line down.
Jack (deadpan): That is the assumption the field runs on. Let us see.
@set bet = monotonic
-> humans

== bet_language
@show You right (thinking)
You: JavaScript and Python will not break the same way.
Jack (smile): A hedge. Fair.
@set bet = language
-> humans

== humans
@chart range Human accuracy by tier | all languages={human_l0}..{human_l3} | JavaScript={js_l0}..{js_l3} | Python under renaming={py_l0}..{py_rename} unit=%
Jack: Overall, accuracy fell from {human_l0} at L0 to {human_l3} at L3. JavaScript declined steadily, {js_l0} to {js_l3}.
Jack: Python went the other way under renaming: {py_l0} to {py_rename}, with L3 easier than L2. Difficulty is non-monotonic and language-specific. ^§4
@if bet == monotonic -> react_monotonic
You: So the language mattered after all.
-> models

== react_monotonic
You: Python got *easier*?
Jack: Under renaming, yes. No one had measured it with people.
-> models

== models
@bg server
@hide You
@show Model right (puzzled)
Model: And where did we struggle?
Jack: Take a variable named `_lastNSecs` that actually tracks recursion depth. It baits a fast, confident, wrong answer: a Stroop trap. ^§2.2
Jack: Reasoning-tuned models tracked human task-level difficulty, Spearman {rho_reason}. Coder- and instruction-tuned models did not, {rho_other}, whatever their size.
Model (thinking): So the most accurate model was not the most human-like.
Jack: That is the paper in one sentence. And alignment means shared difficulty, not shared mechanism: no model reproduced the Python renaming gain. ^§4
Model (deadpan): The longer I think, the worse I do. {rho_cot}.
Jack (smile): Which is why the question was never whether you fail, but whether you fail where we do.
@end
